{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/874668-magickal-exotic-metals-substances","id":874668,"title":"Magickal/Exotic Metals & Substances","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-04-26T10:42:31-07:00","id":874668,"title":"Magickal/Exotic Metals & Substances","content":"While mining my Mage collection for stuff to sprinkle into the next arc of my game (as one does), I noticed something in <i>Storyteller's Companion (Rev.)</i> that sent me down a rabbit hole: what all kinds of specifically named (i.e., canonical) special materials, metals, and substances can one make in <i>Mage: The Ascension</i>?<br /> <br /> What sent me on this search for answers was a Hermetic rote mentioned in regard to the crafting of Wonders, <b><u>The Aura Adamantium</u> </b>(<i>ST Comp. Rev.</i>, p.62).<br /> <br /> I won't post the <i>full</i> description (because it's not all relevant), but in short, <b><u>The Aura Adamantium</u></b> is a <b>Matter 3</b> <i>or </i><b>Matter 5</b> rote, the latter of which is described as follows:<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Matter 5 variant alloys the object with Adamantium, the strongest substance known to legend or comic book, and prevents destruction by all but similar extremely magical effects. </div> </div> </div> No further description is given. I'm curious to know how others treat this substance in their own games--a substance which the the writers of the <i>Storyteller's Companion </i>decided to name with a capital-A (which I hold as significant).<br /> <br /> Mind you, I've known about this rote for a while, and made a system for its implementation (i.e., what the in-game effects of Adamntium are) a while back. I run what has become a fairly cinematic game--there isn't a combat encounter every session, but when there is, my players and I all like a fair bit of crunch, and there just isn't very much crunch to having something that is practically an immovable object. In other words, based just on that narrative description above, there isn't much guidance as to what happens when a bullet, sword, claws, explosion, missiles, fireballs, or deathray hits this substance. I could have ruled that it stops anything short of a 5th Rank magical attack as a matter of course, but frankly seemed lacking, and I'd still be left with the quandry as to <i>how</i> the Adamantium would interact with any given 5th Rank effect. My solution was/is (in short) to have Adamantium confer automatic soak successes (i.e., damage negation) when used as a shield or armor, and automatic damage successes when used as a weapon. Practically speaking, the offensive variant works thus: if a character hits with a Str+2 sword, the [+2] would simply count as automatic successes, but the player still rolls their Strength and any ramp; in the case of a firearm, the damage rating for the weapon is applied as automatic successes, and again, the ramp is rolled separately. Ultimately, it makes the whole thing more easily defeated than perhaps the narrative contemplates, but overall I think it's more fair and even interesting this way. (but that's just me--I can totally see someone making this the most special thing ever and literally nothing can destroy it).<br /> <br /> However, I decided to scour the books for other instances of &quot;special&quot; or &quot;exotic&quot; materials and metals and like. I found a handful of mentions of things, but only a handful had proper systems for their handling. Here's what I found:<br /> <br /> <b>Primium: </b>We all know what this is. I'm not going to bother going too far into it because it's mentioned so often in the books. I'm 90% certain it's either in one of the Iteration X books, or <i>Sons of Ether (Rev.)</i><br /> <br /> <b>Antimatter:</b> this is definitely in <i>Sons of Ether (Rev.)</i>, and it's basically an effect with a great whopping threshold (I wanna say that you don't get jack until you hit 24 or 25 successes total) at which point you get a small amount of material (maybe a gram) that, if not properly contained within and EM field of some kind, will explode causing 3 levels of aggravated damage to everything within a 100 yard radius; every success after that adds another 100 yards and 3 agg. levels to the effect.<br /> <br /> <b>Spider silk</b><b>: </b>This is in <i>Forged by Dragon's Fire</i> near the beginning of the book under &quot;Quick and Dirty 'Artifacts'&quot; on page 17. It's not so much a named substance, as much as an effect one presumably casts on clothes. The effect is simple: damage category reduction (i.e., Agg becomes lethal; lethal becomes bashing).<br /> <br /> Then there are a handful of things mentioned in <i>Mage: The Ascension (2 ed.)</i> on page 204 that have <i>no</i> description or system--they're mentioned just once in the introduction to Matter, and then never again (that I know of). The paragraph in question provides, in pertinent par:<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> [F]inally, at Mastery of the Sphere [the Mage may] create items of wonder and the substances of legend, including <b>Orichalcum </b>(True Gold), <b>Lunargent </b>(True Silver), <b>Hermium </b>(True Mercury)[.] </div> </div> </div> I would think this is a throwaway line, but the fact is that they didn't namecheck these substances at some time prior to their decision to invent and incorporate Primium (the alleged perfected alloy of Gold and Silver) into the game, because the book specifically mentions Primium later on when providing the stats for HIT Mark Vs (&quot;HIT Marks are sheathed in the rare countermagickal alloy Primium.&quot; <i>M:tAs 2ed.</i>, p.277)<br /> <br /> So my questions with regards to these foregoing three substances (i.e., <b>Orichalcum</b>, <b>Lunargent</b>, and <b>Hermium</b>) is: (1) whether there is more written about these anywhere in the classic/old World of Darkness (I know Exalted extensively treats on these or their equivalents); (2) whether anyone has used these in their game(s); (3) what if any special properties are/should be conferred by each; and (4) are there any other <i>Mage</i>-specific (so, no Moonsilver, Sungold, or Soulsteel) &quot;special&quot; or &quot;exotic&quot; materials in the old/classic World of Darkness?<br /> <br /> I look forward to seeing what people have to say about this. Thank you in advance to anyone who contributes--I know this has been a long post.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Sig","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2319-captotter","name":"CaptOtter","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2319_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"405"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-26T14:18:49-07:00","id":874873,"title":"","content":"I believe the best and most correct way to handle the aforementioned situation with the magikal materials is to do whatever any player with a name starting with &quot;J&quot; says to do. It is a fact that people with names starting with J have all the answers (Research Institute of Do What I Say and Don't Question It).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2605-c32ax1","name":"c32ax1","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2605_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"16"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-28T06:02:45-07:00","id":876544,"title":"","content":"Considering the first book I'd go to, Forged By Dragon's Fire, is one you already have, I'm not sure what resources I have access to that you don't. I don't specifically remember much being written about Orichalcum or Hermium (more on Lunargent in a moment). I can say, though, that there probably isn't going to be much. The perfected materials could be special because they occupy a special position in the Tellurian or its cosmology, or they could be special because someone's paradigm says they are special and the high level spheres allow Mages to make things with certain properties. Frequently that decision is left up to the Storyteller to decide.<br /> <br /> Is the Primium that the Technocrats use the same substance as that of legend? They certainly don't think so.<br /> <br /> If you decide that they are special on their own merits though, you may want to rethink looking at other game lines. Lunargent actually translates to Moonsilver, so what Werewolf has to say on that substance would be applicable.<br /> <br /> Edit: Oh, and as a fun aside: Orichalcum may only be associated with Gold due to mistranslation. The first two syllables sound like they derive from Aurum, which is Latin for gold, but they actually derive from Oros, which is Greek for mountain. The full word, in fact, comes from the greek for Mountain Copper.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":758,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","user":"Ramnesis","date":". 04-28-2016, 09:11 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-28T10:01:00-07:00","id":876757,"title":"","content":"And then there's Corinthian Bronze....","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-17T15:13:12-08:00","id":1033676,"title":"","content":"With Matter 5 you can make exotic material... So, you could make <i>adamantium</i>, a metal that doesn't Interact <b>at all</b> with kinetic forces.... So, a bullet would just ricochet, Troile using Potence 10 would explode his/her own hand when punching it. Just like that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12880-karlgust","name":"Karlgust","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"614"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-17T18:16:16-08:00","id":1033778,"title":"","content":"Why would his hand explode? That sounds like it requires a classic Newtonian Third Law kinetic interaction. And it doesn't interact with kinetic forces at all... So wouldn't the correct result be Troile's fist would just stop at the surface of the adamantium? Same with the bullet, and it falls to the ground?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-17T19:18:03-08:00","id":1033819,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CaptOtter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/874668-magickal-exotic-metals-substances#post874668\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Mind you, I've known about this rote for a while, and made a system for its implementation (i.e., what the in-game effects of Adamntium are) a while back. I run what has become a fairly cinematic game--there isn't a combat encounter every session, but when there is, my players and I all like a fair bit of crunch, and there just isn't very much crunch to having something that is practically an immovable object. In other words, based just on that narrative description above, there isn't much guidance as to what happens when a bullet, sword, claws, explosion, missiles, fireballs, or deathray hits this substance. I could have ruled that it stops anything short of a 5th Rank magical attack as a matter of course, but frankly seemed lacking, and I'd still be left with the quandry as to <i>how</i> the Adamantium would interact with any given 5th Rank effect. My solution was/is (in short) to have Adamantium confer automatic soak successes (i.e., damage negation) when used as a shield or armor, and automatic damage successes when used as a weapon. Practically speaking, the offensive variant works thus: if a character hits with a Str+2 sword, the [+2] would simply count as automatic successes, but the player still rolls their Strength and any ramp; in the case of a firearm, the damage rating for the weapon is applied as automatic successes, and again, the ramp is rolled separately. <b>Ultimately, it makes the whole thing more easily defeated than perhaps the narrative contemplates, but overall I think it's more fair and even interesting this way.</b> (but that's just me--I can totally see someone making this the most special thing ever and literally nothing can destroy it).</div> </div> </div> </div>Honestly, I find the idea of &quot;nerfing&quot; materials created with Matter 5 to be missing the point. This is the product of Master level understanding of the Sphere. The products of this creation should be over-the-top, in much the same way that Forces 5 lets the user summon tornadoes, or how Time 5 lets you stop time, Dio Brando style. Making a material that in indestructible by anything less than similarly potent effects should be par for the course.<br /> <br /> That said, just because a sword can't be broken doesn't mean it's also supernaturally sharp (although, with Matter, that's a trivial problem to solve). It could still get stuck in a thick piece of armor or hunk of solid granite. (I don't know enough about physics and blades to know what could stop a blade that is otherwise unbreakable, so correct me if I'm wrong). If that's true, and the weapon isn't any heavier than a regular blade, it won't necessarily do more damage than a common sword of the same shape. Same with an Adamantium bullet, which might resist warping but otherwise be totally affected by the forces acting in opposition to it by the object it hits. (Again, I don't know enough about ballistics to know for sure).<br /> <br /> Perhaps the real value of Adamantium would be in <i>armor</i>. It just <i>will not yield</i> to conventional attacks, giving it ridiculous Durability and Structure. This is where Armor ratings of 4+ come from, as you're relying on the ambient force of the hit to do damage to the target (anyone who has worn a &quot;bullet-proof&quot; vest knows that even if it doesn't pierce your hide, the shots can still knock the wind out of you). It also ignores any rules about excessive force damaging the armor's Structure, since it does not incur damage. Even then, though, it's not that overpowered all things considered. If the soak roll fails, it's obviously because the armor was circumvented, or by shear force against the whole body.<br /> <br /> Really, Vibranium is the overpowered metal, not Adamantium. Being able to perfectly disperse and negate impact force as an inherent physical property of the metal.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-17T19:19:05-08:00","id":1033821,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/874668-magickal-exotic-metals-substances?p=1033778#post1033778\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Why would his hand explode? That sounds like it requires a classic Newtonian Third Law kinetic interaction. And it doesn't interact with kinetic forces at all... So wouldn't the correct result be Troile's fist would just stop at the surface of the adamantium? Same with the bullet, and it falls to the ground?</div> </div> </div> </div>Could be. Or it could just traverse the metal unhindered, creating a big &quot;bang!&quot; behind it in a supersonic explosion, or it could &quot;return to sender&quot; all the energy... After all, what happens when an immovable object meets an irresistable force?<br /> <br /> The energy is not dissipated, because that would require an interaction with the force... That's why I think a &quot;return to sender&quot; would be more fitting. But hey, it's actually something we can't imagine exactly because defies all logic... But that's what being a master means.<br /> <br /> So, feel free to consider it in any way you'd prefer. And since not everything in Mage is about science... Newton may be damned! Or not...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12880-karlgust","name":"Karlgust","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"614"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-17T19:26:43-08:00","id":1033829,"title":"","content":"Yep, make your super hyper ridiculous Hit Mark X, the Hunter of antidiluvians, with one external layer of Adamantium, followed by one of Vibranium and a good dose of Primium inside... Well, goodbye Troile.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12880-karlgust","name":"Karlgust","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"614"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-17T19:29:27-08:00","id":1033833,"title":"","content":"That effect of stoping Troile's fist would probably be with Vibranium, not Adamantium. It would feel is Troile hitted a fog that stopped his punch","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12880-karlgust","name":"Karlgust","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"614"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-18T06:35:11-08:00","id":1033982,"title":"","content":"Now I'm usually a fan of Mage trumps all. Wrong or right is my pet game. However I think that Troile's potence of 9 (shes only 4th right?) Would count as a &quot; similarly powerful effect&quot; to destroy Adamantium. It is a bit concieted to say a rank 9 Discipline is inferior to something that can be created by rank 5 Sphere magic.<br /> <br /> As for Hit Marks on steroids hunting down Antedeluvians, that was comical.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":987,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/987-tuch","user":"Tuch","date":". 01-18-2017, 09:38 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/987-tuch","name":"Tuch","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar987_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-18T08:52:04-08:00","id":1034041,"title":"","content":"She ate her dad. (Who the Assamites ponder might have eaten his dad....) So she's Third Gen at least. So the powers are &quot;not measurable.... ST fiat&quot; level. Troile's Potence is effectively incalculable. She could have &quot;Potence 10 - Unstoppable Force&quot; and be able to force her fist through anything that doesn't have Fortitude 10 (of the right sort) or an equivalent Archmastery Sphere, Incarnae level ability or so on.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-18T08:54:43-08:00","id":1034044,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Karlgust</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/874668-magickal-exotic-metals-substances?p=1033821#post1033821\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Could be. Or it could just traverse the metal unhindered, creating a big &quot;bang!&quot; behind it in a supersonic explosion, or it could &quot;return to sender&quot; all the energy... After all, what happens when an immovable object meets an irresistable force?<br /> <br /> The energy is not dissipated, because that would require an interaction with the force... That's why I think a &quot;return to sender&quot; would be more fitting. But hey, it's actually something we can't imagine exactly because defies all logic... But that's what being a master means.<br /> <br /> So, feel free to consider it in any way you'd prefer. And since not everything in Mage is about science... Newton may be damned! Or not...</div> </div> </div> </div>If Adamantium ignore all kinetic forces, it can't even be moved...<br /> <br /> And &quot;kinetic forces&quot; are ALL about science. Once you establish your paradigm, it guides the discussion. If it wasn't &quot;adamantium&quot; but &quot;adamant&quot; as described in the Greek myths, this would have to be framed differently. Unless it was a &quot;fist clothed in adamantium&quot; vs. a &quot;shield of adamant&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-18T09:37:38-08:00","id":1034077,"title":"","content":"&quot;Kinect forces&quot; is the name we use in our real world to understand and classify it, because Im not an expert in exoteric geometry to say it is the &quot;mercuriam displacement of the elements following aureum water proporcions&quot;, nor Im I an Extatic to call it &quot;singular notion of the alteration of perceptions uppon succesive transitions of ones shakras&quot;.<br /> <br /> What you said holds true for the Technocracy, but to no ones else.... I mean, an Hermetic may (and probably will) know the notions of kinetic forces that the Union spoon feed him before his awakening... But now, &quot;he knows better&quot;. And he may even call it &quot;kinetic forces&quot; for pratical use, but he knows that it truly is the &quot;mercuriam&quot; yadda yadda yadda","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12880-karlgust","name":"Karlgust","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"614"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-18T11:56:16-08:00","id":1034148,"title":"","content":"Consider what Mage: the Awakening says about perfected metals. <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0BxveHUKxwBU9RnlfTkJnY2NyMjQ/view\" target=\"_blank\">Here's</a> a draft version of what'll be published in one of the 2nd edition books soon. The basic approach they took was to choose one of the thematic aspects of the metal - something that makes it different than other metals - and extrapolate it. So just as gold is especially unlikely to chemically react with something else, perfected gold is supernaturally resistant to corrosion. Just as silver is the color of mirrors that allow you to see other perspectives, mirrors made with perfected silver allow you to see into the spirit world. And so on.<br /> <br /> You could steal the basic concept for your game, choosing thematic aspects of the nonmagical materials for the perfected version to exemplify. What's the most interesting thing about lead? About tin? About copper? About bronze?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7356-haldir2012","name":"haldir2012","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"148"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-20T09:46:23-08:00","id":1035278,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>haldir2012</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/874668-magickal-exotic-metals-substances?p=1034148#post1034148\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Consider what Mage: the Awakening says about perfected metals. <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0BxveHUKxwBU9RnlfTkJnY2NyMjQ/view\" target=\"_blank\">Here's</a> a draft version of what'll be published in one of the 2nd edition books soon. The basic approach they took was to choose one of the thematic aspects of the metal - something that makes it different than other metals - and extrapolate it. So just as gold is especially unlikely to chemically react with something else, perfected gold is supernaturally resistant to corrosion. Just as silver is the color of mirrors that allow you to see other perspectives, mirrors made with perfected silver allow you to see into the spirit world. And so on.<br /> <br /> You could steal the basic concept for your game, choosing thematic aspects of the nonmagical materials for the perfected version to exemplify. What's the most interesting thing about lead? About tin? About copper? About bronze?</div> </div> </div> </div>THESE are exotic metals for Hermetics (and Solificati), not &quot;adamantium&quot;.<br /> <br /> Adamantium is a sci-fi metal. It's going to be a Union thing (It-X creation &amp; distributed appropriately) or a SoE thing. In either case, it's going to work by scientific/quasi-scientific principles. So, if there's some &quot;no kinetic energy&quot; tag attached, that because &quot;kinetic energy&quot; is a science-y thing and appropriate to the description of the substance in question. <br /> <br /> But guess what? What &quot;kinetic energy&quot; means in a techological/-matic paradigm has analogies elsewhere, even if they aren't even considered explicit concept. So, &quot;adamantium&quot; is going to have some effect no matter what paradigm (Old meaning... we REALLY need a word for this concept that has been edited out of Mage20 in favor of Focus even though they are NOT the same thing at all) someone coming up against adamantium is using, it's going to work. Just like a technocratic &quot;heat suppression system&quot; will work against someone manipulating &quot;elemental fire&quot;. So, if adamantium &quot;stops all kinetic energy&quot; then there is going to be some analogy that will work for an Akashic (it's elemental Metal infused with pure yang qi that makes it obdurate), or a Hermetic of a less alchemical bent (it denies the harmonies of Platonic motion as an idealization that keeps the essential movement of the Spheres) and so on.<br /> <br /> I'm not sure where adamantium ever became associated with &quot;anti-kinesis&quot; though, ever since the term was coined in <i>Forbidden Planet</i> it's just been a description of a really, really hard metal. <br /> <br /> I guess &quot;Adamant&quot; could fit under the paradigm above as a quintessential sort of steel, as one of the attributed prized about steel, over purer iron, would be hardness, if you want to go through the &quot;mystic refinement&quot; process that can produce &quot;true gold&quot; or &quot;true silver&quot; or whatever<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}